{title: OKIE FROM MUSKOGEE}
{subtitle: Merle Haggard}
{start_of_verse: Intro}
[A7]       [A7]       [D]

We don't smoke marijuana in Muskogee.   We don't take our trips on L.S. [A7]D.
We don't burn our draft cards down on Main Street.   Cuz we like livin' right, and bein' [D]free.

We don't make a party out of lovin'.   But we like holdin' hands and pitchin' [A7]woo.
We don't let our hair grow long and [A]shaggy, like the hippies out in San Francisco [D]do.

And I'm proud to be an Okie from Muskogee.   A place where even squares can have a [A]ball.
[A7]We still wave Old Glory down at the courthouse, and white [A]lightnin's still the biggest thrill of [D]all.

[(][capo][+1][)]     [D]

Leather boots are still in style for manly footwear.   Beads and Roman sandals won't be [A7]seen.
And football's still the roughest thing on [A]campus, and the kids here still respect the college [D]dean.

And I'm proud to be an Okie from Muskogee.   A place where even squares can have a [A]ball.
